376 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 158 0 R
/E (We chose this approach instead of the full LLM approach, or the full rule-based approach. The complete rule-based approach would be speedy, predictable and easy to work out, but it would stumble on the literary language which does not conform neatly to the rules of grammar. The complete LLM approach would be potentially very costly to run on a whole novel, and would produce a wildly variable pattern of output. By splitting the work between spaCy for the speedy offline process \(tokenisation, NER, sentence splitting\), and Gemini for the more philosophically heavyweight work \(relationship classifying, summarising and reviewing aliases\), we get the best of both worlds.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
377 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 158 0 R
/E (Another initial and deliberate decision was to use Streamlit as the front-end. We could have used React to create a front-end but we figured that for a project of this size, this would eat up too much of our time to work on the front-end instead of on the NLP work that is our core contribution. Using Streamlit meant that we could create a halfway decent front-end without spending weeks working on it.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 158 0 R
/E (The remaining part of the report is organized as follows. Section 2 includes the literature review of the field with existing tools. Section 3 gives the formal definition of the problem and list the research questions. Section 4 includes the description of approach and system architecture. Section 5 elaborates algorithms of the NLP core pipeline. Section 6 includes the system flowcharts, diagrams of the data flow. Section 7 includes implementation details and tech stack. Section 8 provides testing results of Dracula and comparison with the existing related work. Section 9 includes limitations and possible future work. Section 10 includes discussion of a list of references according to the IEEE format.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
