426 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (This is a fairly new technique for Large Language Models in NLP pipelines. For example, the large context window that models in the Gemini API [5], which was used in this project, have allows an entire chapter or even a short novel to be submitted in one call. In the past, transformer models such as BERT [1] had a maximum context of 512 tokens, which led to the necessity of chunking schemes.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
427 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (The primary benefit for Lit Mind is that with the Gemini API, you are getting structured output generated through Pydantic schema enforcement. As long as you specify the exact JSON schema of the output you want, you can pull that data out of the model's output much more reliably \(by, say, exact character name, relation type, and sentiment score\) than our earlier LLM-based literary analysis system that relied on regex parsing of the text response.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
428 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (The graph library networkX [7], a second data structure used in Lit Mind, is built at Los Alamos National Laboratory and was first described by Hagberg, Swart, and Chult \(2008\). It offers optimized implementations of common graph algorithms such as degree, betweenness, and closeness centrality,)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
429 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (as well as Page Rank and community detection, that have all been used for character networks by various authors.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
430 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (Newman [8] demonstrated that in many actual social networks a person's betweenness centrality can be a more accurate measure of importance than a person's degree centrality \(number of friends\). In character networks, this can indicate a character who sits between two disconnected groups, such as a messenger who is mentioned by both the hero's and the villain's groups, has high betweenness centrality but not necessarily a high mention count. We calculate both metrics so that users can see both perspectives.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
431 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (VADER \(Valence Aware Dictionary and sEntiment Reasoner\) and Text Blob, the two algorithms used here, were designed for short social media text and product reviews. They tend to run into difficulties when analyzing longer passages of literary prose, where words derive much of their positive or negative meaning from their ironic, metaphorical and allusory contexts. For instance, the algorithms tend to interpret a positive score for "I shall follow him to the grave," just because the word "follow" appears.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
432 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (Here are some examples that have been tried. Nalisnick and Baird [5], for example, tried to plot the arcs of character sentiment throughout a novel by scoring sentiment on the text where each character appears, and plotting those scores as they change over time. Not a perfect solution, but it does produce interesting - maybe not so granulated, but still interesting - visualizations of how the world of a character is altered. Comparison: relationship scores in Lit Mind work the same way: They are scored from the perspective of a pair of characters that co-occur in a passage, not from all of them combined.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
