/P 175 0 R
/E (Lit Mind operated on a selection of ubiquitous classics with regard to William Shakespeare's Hamlet as well as Bram Stoker's Dracula which the group described as training data that have been human enabled to find and label.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
786 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (There were two test novels. Bram Stoker's Dracula \(1897\) was taken from Project Gutenberg as a plain text file. The file is 865,207 characters, approximately 8,561 sentences, and 208,854 tokens in length. It is a good test case since it has a limited and known set of named characters with well documented relationships and it can be taken as ground truth.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
787 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes Arthur Conan Doyle \(1892\). A different structural difficulty in short stories from the past, as this is a collection of short stories, the cast is not typical for most of the stories so the system must be flexible to the fact that everyone can only appear in a few of the stories, apart from Holmes and Watson.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
788 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
